    <div class="cs-eyebrow">(08) — Take-aways</div>
    <h2 class="cs-h2" style="margin:0 0 40px;">What I’m taking forward</h2>
    <div class="cs-grid-3" style="gap:36px;">
      <div>
        <div style="height:3px;width:40px;background:var(--color-accent);margin-bottom:18px;"></div>
        <div style="font-weight:700;font-size:19px;margin-bottom:10px;">Change the concept, not just the canvas</div>
        <p class="cs-info-p" style="font-size:15px;">My biggest improvement wasn’t a screen — it was renaming Habits to Bonds. When testing keeps pointing somewhere, the brave move is to re-frame the core idea, not just polish the one you started with.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div style="height:3px;width:40px;background:var(--color-navy);margin-bottom:18px;"></div>
        <div style="font-weight:700;font-size:19px;margin-bottom:10px;">Subtract for the real user</div>
        <p class="cs-info-p" style="font-size:15px;">I cut the data-heavy stats pages. They demoed well, but my users tracked casually through journaling and didn’t want them. Designing for the actual persona meant removing features, not adding them.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div style="height:3px;width:40px;background:var(--color-accent-warm);margin-bottom:18px;"></div>
        <div style="font-weight:700;font-size:19px;margin-bottom:10px;">What I’d test sooner</div>
        <p class="cs-info-p" style="font-size:15px;">I validated the screens, but not the social loop itself. Next time I’d put the core accountability mechanic in front of a real friend group before refining UI — the product lives or dies on that behavior, not the pixels.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
